 because  um  like you said it was a police officer  that stopped her that abused  abused you know his power as a police  officer and i think  there's so  i think we've been reflecting so much  about how it could have been any one of  us and the fact is that it was one of us  every time that the 101 women who have  died at the hands of male violence since  the death of sarah everard  who perhaps we haven't had that same  energy and and outrage and uproar for  it was it was one of us every time and  in in conditions as as as gruesome and  as tragic as hers and that's not to take  away from what happened to sarah of  course but  um it really makes you reflect and as an  organization we're try we're trying to  be there for as many people as we can  um  but obviously uh our resources are  limited compared to an institution like  the government  um and i think there needs to be  fundamental institutional change to  protect women to protect marginalized  groups  um because unfortunately this this will  keep happening and i think there's a  societal problem as well in the way that  men see women and often the way in which  women's lives of you know viewed as  expandable  um  and and i think we we need to have a lot  of  it needs to do a lot of work to reform  the way in which women are viewed in  society from a young age  sure there are apps available of course  that uh can can basically walk you home  a call center uh as a live app on on  your phone you can call people if you  need them those are coming on stream all  the time but what's your advice to to  women particularly who who are listening  this morning about how to try to keep  that little bit safer when they're  potentially walking home or even getting  out of their car at night on their own  i think it's so difficult to offer that  advice because  so many women live in the knowledge that  whatever precautions you might take  whether it's  calling a friend whether it's calling  strength safe whether it's  keeping your keys between your fingers  whether it's picking a different route  whether it's only only a short walk you  know only five minutes across the park  which was the walk that sabine nessa  took and unfortunately didn't survive  um  whatever advice i might offer  whatever advice any of us might offer  i don't think it's enough because  sometimes all it's doing  is saying make sure  make sure he attacks the other girl  make sure it's not me  and whatever precaution you might take  like you say at 9pm in the evening it's  not  it's not so late that nobody's about  is it going to be enough  and i don't say that  to put any responsibility on women  i say that to put the responsibility on  the people who attack and kill these  women  because it's their responsibility and  women should never ever be made  responsible for the actions that are  committed against them
 

